## The Bug

The problem is on the line `fmt.Println(i)` inside the anonymous function. In Go (prior to 1.22), a closure captures **variables by reference**, not by value. The anonymous function doesn't get its own copy of `i` — it holds a pointer to the single loop variable `i` in the `for` statement.

By the time any of the goroutines actually get scheduled and execute `fmt.Println(i)`, the `for` loop has already finished and `i` has been incremented to `5`. All five goroutines read the same variable, which now holds `5`.

## The Fix

Pass `i` as a parameter to the goroutine so each one captures its own **value** at the time the goroutine is spawned:

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			fmt.Println(i)
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

The only change is `go func(i int) { ... }(i)` — the loop variable is evaluated *now* (at each iteration) and passed by value into the goroutine's own parameter `i`. Each goroutine now sees the value that was current when it was launched, producing output `0 1 2 3 4` (in non-deterministic order).

> **Note:** Go 1.22 changed loop variable semantics so that each iteration gets a fresh variable, which would make your original code work as-is. But since you're on Go 1.21, the explicit parameter-passing fix above is the correct approach.

